.TH PINBOARD 4
.SH NAME
pinboard \- desktop control and state file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
echo 'pin /Apps/!Manual' >/chan/pinboard
cat /chan/pinboard
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pinboard
(see
.IR pinboard (1))
uses
.IR sys-file2chan (2)
to serve the synthetic file
.BR /chan/pinboard .
Programs and shell scripts use this file to add and remove desktop objects,
set the backdrop, and save or restore the current layout.
There is no separate Pinboard Limbo module: the file protocol is its public
programming interface.
.PP
The file accepts a line-oriented textual protocol.
Fields are separated by spaces or tabs and paths must not contain white space.
A command may be split across several writes; it takes effect when its newline
is received.
Unknown commands and malformed or inapplicable commands are ignored.
The commands are:
.TP
.BI pin " path" " [x y]"
Add an icon for
.IR path .
The path must exist.
If
.I x
and
.I y
are supplied, they give the centre of the icon in screen pixels.
The coordinates are clamped to keep the icon and caption on the desktop.
If they are omitted or negative, the next vacant grid position is chosen.
More than one icon can refer to the same path.
.TP
.BI unpin " path"
Remove every icon referring to
.IR path .
.TP
.BI backdrop " image"
Replace the backdrop with
.IR image .
The image must be an Inferno
.B .bit
image.  It is drawn at the desktop origin and is not scaled.
If the image cannot be opened, the existing backdrop is unchanged.
.TP
.B clear
Remove all pinned icons without changing the backdrop.
.PP
Reading the file returns a snapshot in the same command language: first a
.B backdrop
line, when one is set, followed by one
.B pin
line for every icon, including its coordinates.
A read beginning at offset zero captures fresh state; subsequent reads at
increasing offsets return the remainder of that same snapshot.
Consequently a snapshot can be saved and later written back to reconstruct a
layout.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add an application using automatic placement:
.IP
.EX
echo 'pin /Apps/!Manual' >/chan/pinboard
.EE
.PP
Place a file at a particular location:
.IP
.EX
echo 'pin /usr/inferno/notes 120 96' >/chan/pinboard
.EE
.PP
Save a layout, then replace the current icons with that saved layout:
.IP
.EX
cat /chan/pinboard >/tmp/pinboard.state
echo clear >/chan/pinboard
cat /tmp/pinboard.state >/chan/pinboard
.EE
.PP
A Limbo client can use the ordinary
.IR Sys
file operations:
.IP
.EX
fd := sys->open("/chan/pinboard", Sys->OWRITE);
if(fd == nil)
	raise "fail:pinboard";
cmd := array of byte "pin /Apps/!Manual\en";
if(sys->write(fd, cmd, len cmd) != len cmd)
	raise "fail:pinboard write";
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /chan/pinboard
The control and state file.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/pinboard.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pinboard (1),
.IR sys-file2chan (2),
.IR srv (3),
.IR plumbing (6),
.IR plumber (8)
.SH BUGS
The protocol has no quoting or escaping convention, so paths containing spaces
cannot be used.
.PP
State is not written to permanent storage automatically.

